# Workflow

```text
Grill (when the design branches) → Issue → branch → work → PR → human review → squash merge → retrospective
```

1. Grill when the API, the meaning, or a milestone boundary branches. Skill: `.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md`. Skip it when the Issue already has acceptance text and the design settles the question.
2. One Issue is one PR. Create the Issue from a template in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`. Blank issues are off.
3. A change to the meaning of the spec is a `design` Issue that updates `docs/design.ja.md` before any implementation Issue. The Japanese file is the spec. `docs/design.ja.md` stays a draft until a design Issue says it is the contract.
4. Acceptance text lives on the Issue. The roadmap keeps ID, title, Issue, and status only.
5. Branch from `main` after a fetch: `git switch -c <branch> origin/main`. `git switch -c` alone starts from the branch that is checked out. The name is `type/<issue-number>-<slug>`. `type` is a Conventional Commits type.
6. Open the PR in the same turn that the branch is pushed. Use `.github/pull_request_template.md`. Write `Closes #N` on its own line under `## Related`.
7. A human reviews and squash-merges. An agent never merges.
8. After the merge, delete the local and remote branch. A withdrawn prototype's branch is not a merged branch: post its diff on the Issue and leave the branch until the owner names it.
9. Retrospective, below, before the next feature branch.

## Gates

- `just lint`: `ruff format --check`, `ruff check`, and `pyright`.
- `just test`: `pytest`.
- `just coverage`: branch coverage of at least 90%. Raising the threshold is a new Issue.

CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs the three gates on Ubuntu and Windows. After each merge, read `main`'s CI. A red `main` gets its own Issue and PR before the next merge.

## Forbidden

- Starting implementation without an Issue number and a branch. The convention bootstrap was ordered in chat before Issues existed; the next change is not that exception.
- Copying acceptance text onto the roadmap.
- Starting a roadmap row that reads `Set after Grill on #n`.
- Adding or removing roadmap rows, or touching **Intentionally out of scope**, without a Grill.
- Merging. Closing an Issue or PR without an imperative that names it.
- Ending a turn on a branch that has no Issue and no PR, or leaving files you created that are no longer needed.

## Numbers and language

Create an Issue before its number is written anywhere. Issue and pull request numbers share one sequence. A PR body is written after `gh issue create` has returned the number.

Identifiers, docstrings, README, and the package description are English. Issue bodies, PR bodies, and commit subjects may be Japanese. `type`, `scope`, and the PR template headings stay English. The spec is `docs/design.ja.md`.

Before calling a PR ready, and whenever the owner asks for the next step, read the reviews and inline comments of every open PR.

## Retrospective

After a squash merge, in the turn that learns of it, write three points as a comment on the merged PR. Write "none" when a point is empty.

1. A lesson for `.cursor/rules/`, a skill, `docs/design.ja.md`, or one ADR under `docs/adr/`. Tacit knowledge the work relied on is a lesson.
2. The Issue that will carry it, if any.
3. Whether to refactor. Yes opens a `refactor` Issue that is next, before the next feature. Postponing it needs a roadmap row that names when.

Then, without waiting for an ask, file a `chore` Issue and one PR that writes each lesson of point 1 into the file it names. The next feature branch waits until that PR is open. There is no retrospective log.

A correction given in conversation lands in the rule it belongs to in the same change. A decision that needs its reason kept becomes an ADR.
